Heres a nifty idea,

Container:
Saddle bag

appearance: buckled sack
cost: 100 zorkmids
weight: 40-60
prob: 5 (same as saddle)

when applied:

Your sack is empty. --more--
Do you wish to put something in? [ynq] (n)

[if yes, allow for inventory stashing]

Apply in which direction?

[applied to a square w/o a creature]

Nothing happens. 
[maybe...]
You drop the <buckled sack/saddle bag> next to you.

[applied to a non saddle-compatible monster]

I don't think the <monster> would appreciate that.
[or]
You can't equip such a creature
[when (a)pplying a saddle to a newt, says: "You can't saddle such 
a creature"]

[if applied to a saddle-compatible creature, check the saddle 
code, if a saddle could be sucessfully applied, so could the 
saddle bags]

You strap the <buckled sack/saddle bags> to the <mount>.


Whether in your inventory or on the back of a pet, the saddle bag 
would have the same capacity as a sack. I.e. no cramming chests 
and boxes into it. Using the #loot command, if you point at your 
pack animal you'd see:

You see a bag on your <mount>, loot it? [ynq] (n)

[if yes, allow inv stashing, etc...] then:

Do you want to remove the saddle bag from the <mount>? [ynq] (n)
[and if applicable]
Do you want to remove the saddle from the <mount>? [ynq] (n)

I figure a mount could wear the saddle and the saddle bags 
simultaneously. If you were mounted, #looting would be the same 
as if you were standing over a sack. If you were above a chest 
(and were skilled enough to loot it) it'd be equivalent to 
standing on a square with a sack and chest.

It would allow quite a bit of extra carrying capacity for sure, 
but no more unbalanced than a blessed bag of holding, and 
certainly harder to maintain. u's are hard to feed, and other 
saddle-able animals are tough to tame. Also, the saddle bag would 
be vulnerable to disintegration (i lost a mount and a saddle to a 
black dragon's breath once).  I think it would add an interesting 
dimension to inventory management (which is arguably the most 
important mid/late-game challenge of nethack), as well as a fun, 
useful, TDTTOE tool.

whaddya think?

